  "query_preview": "..\n/Issued 1236 AM MDT Wed Aug 5 2026/ \n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nNorthwest flow aloft continues over the next few days as an upper\nlevel ridge begins to build off of the West coast. The gradual \nbuilding of this ridge will cause temperatures to increase, \npeaking Friday and Saturday with highs in the low to mid 90s for \nmuch of the CWA. Precipitable water remains low and forcing \nremains weak, keeping the region dry throughout the week into the \nweekend. \n\nOn Friday, the ridge begins to flatten, resulting in increased \nwinds aloft. Daytime mixing will mix these higher winds down to \nthe surface, creating breezy conditions Friday and Saturday. The \nmixing will also lower humidities, with minimum humidities \nreaching the teens across the region during this time. The warm \ntemperatures, breezy conditions, and low humidities will create \nelevated to near critical fire weather conditions. The beginning \nof next week will see westerly flow aloft, with low end chances \nfor precipitation. -Dzomba\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nOne thing that remains uncertain is the timing and location of \nthe breezier winds Friday. Some solutions have a broader 700mb jet\nFriday afternoon, some have a more localized jet, and some push \noff the breezier winds to Saturday. This affects the overall fire \nweather risk threat. However, there remains good confidence for at\nleast more elevated fire weather Friday and Saturday due to the \ncombination of dry, hot, and breezy conditions. Where the better \nwinds set up will determine the areas more at risk for critical \nfire weather. -Wilson",
